WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.509
I'm at the Computer History Museum in Mountain
View, California,

00:00:03.509 --> 00:00:07.279
and amongst all the thousands of artifacts
here is this:

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:09.220
a regular, everyday teapot.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.730
The world's most famous teapot.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.980
It's called the Utah Teapot, and you've almost
certainly seen it before,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.129
even if you don't realise it.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:17.770
Or at least, a digital version of it.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.820
In the old Windows Pipes screensaver,

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.480
occasionally instead of a ball joint,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.940
a teapot would appear.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:23.940
This teapot.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.070
It's in the background of that 3D-animated
Simpsons episode from the 90s,

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.039
and it's in a couple of Pixar films.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:30.670
It's an in-joke amongst animators,

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.780
and that's because it was the first realistic,
complex object

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.539
to be widely used in computer graphics.

00:00:37.539 --> 00:00:42.670
In 1974, a researcher at the University of
Utah called Martin Newell

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.300
needed a reference object, something simple
to test the algorithms he was designing.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.639
The story goes that he told his wife this
as they were sitting down for tea --

00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:53.149
Newell was born in Britain, so tea --

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:55.260
and she suggested that modelling the teapot.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.949
And it was ideal.

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:58.909
It has concave and convex surfaces.

00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:00.879
It can cast shadows on itself,

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:04.460
which is a problem that some algorithms might
find difficult to solve.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.270
It's immediately recognisable:

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.600
you can tell by eye if the teapot looks like
a teapot,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.840
or if something's gone wrong with your rendering.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.360
And you don't have to texture it to make it
look good.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.310
As long as it has a plain, light colour, and
it...

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.159
well, it... it looks like a teapot.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:21.370
But it's also not too complicated: back in
the seventies,

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.640
computer graphics involved a lot of working
by hand.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.299
Newell sketched the teapot on graph paper,

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:28.510
and then typed in the resulting coordinates
by hand.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.690
And here's what I really like about the teapot:

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.079
over the forty-something years that the digital
version of it has existed,

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:36.330
details about it have been lost to history.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.540
It's said that it was used so much for early
graphics experiments

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.399
that some people could remember all of its
data in their head

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:43.270
and just type it in when they needed it.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.330
Can I find a reliable source for that?

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:45.390
Absolutely not,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.810
but it's a great story.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.159
And at some point, the teapot got squished
a bit:

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:52.869
some people claim that it's because of old
displays with non-square pixels,

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:56.830
and some claim it's just because it looked
better that way.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:00.850
This is a artifact of the digital era that's
got urban legends around it.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.340
And whether they're true or not,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.430
there's now a canonical version of the teapot:

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:10.550
a digital object that's been copied and copied
and copied into all sorts of modern culture.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:14.940
Literally billions of people have seen -- well,
not this teapot --

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.760
but a version of it that can be described
in one page of numbers.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.050
Thanks very much to the Computer History Museum
in Mountain View, California!

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.551
Pull down the description for links to them
and the amazing exhibits they've got here.

